<h1>Web accessibility audit and training</h1>
<p class="lede">Four ways to work with tuaccesible, depending on what you already know about your product and how much time you have.</p>

<p>The manual testing is done by María Pía, who has used a screen reader as her main working tool for more than thirteen years. That changes what gets tested: a form is worked through the way someone who depends on that technology to complete it works through it, not the way someone who switches the screen reader on to check it does. And no report goes out on a single reading: another person on the team reviews the findings and their priority before they reach you. You can read about <a href="/en/team/">who audits your site</a>.</p>

<p>We quote every project based on its complexity, scope, and estimated time. No public price list, because no two sites are the same.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Express audit</h2>
  <p>The fast web accessibility diagnosis, so you know where you stand before committing to a bigger project. We review the home page and a selection of key links. Delivered in 2 to 3 business days.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the express audit includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>A manual review of the home page and a selection of key links.</li>
        <li>Direct observations, ordered by what has the most impact.</li>
        <li>A concrete recommendation on where to go next.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What it doesn't include:</strong> the page-by-page report or the full criteria table. That's what the site audit adds.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Get a quote for an express audit</a></p>

  <h2>Site audit</h2>
  <p>The foundation for any real accessibility statement. <strong>We evaluate the site page by page and link by link, not a sample.</strong> The usual practice in this industry is to audit a representative sample of around 8 to 15 pages; here everything gets reviewed, against Level AA as the minimum.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the site audit includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>Evaluation page by page and link by link, not a sample.</li>
        <li>Level AA as the minimum, with AAA suggested wherever it's achievable.</li>
        <li>A full report with criteria table, screenshots, and examples.</li>
        <li>Both sides of the audit: the functional one, with keyboard and screen reader, and the visual one too.</li>
        <li>A closing call to go through the findings with whoever you want on your team.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>How it differs from the express audit:</strong> the express audit tells you whether you have a problem. This one tells you exactly which, where, and at what priority, in a document that works as the basis for an accessibility statement.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Get a quote for a site audit</a></p>

  <h2>Full package</h2>
  <p>Everything in the site audit, plus what it takes for the fixes to actually get implemented. It's the only option with support after delivery.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the full package includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>Everything in the site audit.</li>
        <li>Follow-up after delivery, while your team implements.</li>
        <li>The accessibility statement already drafted.</li>
        <li>A discount on the second review, once the changes are in place.</li>
        <li>A live audit session, 30 minutes to an hour and a half, depending on what there is to show and discuss.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The full report is guaranteed regardless of how that live session goes. We hand the recording to you as a file, with the captions in a separate file, so you can watch it in whatever player you already use and keep it wherever you want. It is not published anywhere, and it does not sit on any channel of ours.</p>
      <p><strong>How it differs from the site audit:</strong> the audit ends when you receive the report. The full package ends when the changes are done.</p>
      <p>If you'd rather not handle it in-house, we also assess and quote the remediation: we apply the changes in the code, not just flag them.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Ask about the full package</a></p>

  <h2>Corporate workshop</h2>
  <p>Web accessibility training so your team stops repeating the same mistakes on every new project. A one-hour session, extendable as needed. Remote anywhere, or in person in Argentina. For now we run workshops in Spanish only.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the corporate workshop includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>A presentation built around your team's specific questions.</li>
        <li>A live demonstration navigating with a screen reader, as the centerpiece of the session.</li>
        <li>A mixed audience: design, development, and content in the same room, building shared vocabulary across roles.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The demonstration works far better on your own site than on a generic example.</p>
      <p><strong>How it differs from the rest:</strong> the other three services evaluate a product. This one trains the people who build it, so the next one is right from the start.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Ask about a workshop</a></p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>How we audit web accessibility</h2>
  <p>We combine automated tools with manual review using real screen readers: NVDA, JAWS, Windows Narrator, and TalkBack on Android. Automation gives a first pass. Manual testing is what finds the problems that matter, and there are no shortcuts there.</p>
  <p>We work from WCAG 2.2 as the common baseline. From there we evaluate against whichever framework applies to you: EN 301 549 and the European Accessibility Act, the EAA, in Europe; ADA and Section 508 in the United States; or the relevant national regulation in Latin America. If you are not sure which one covers you, we explain it in <a href="/en/regulations/">which accessibility law applies to you</a>.</p>
  <p>We're not limited to websites. We audit Android apps, PDF and EPUB documents, desktop software, and browser extensions.</p>
  <p>Every report adapts to who's going to read it. It can be delivered in three formats:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A full document, with criteria table, screenshots, and examples.</li>
    <li>A spreadsheet, if you need the complete technical detail.</li>
    <li>A plain-language version, for the rest of the team.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We don't stop at flagging the problem: we propose the fix.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Working terms</h2>
  <p>We work with individual clients and with agencies, with a preference for direct clients. A 50% deposit is due once the contract is signed, in writing or after a video call, with the rest due on delivery.</p>
  <p>We accept PayPal and bank transfer from Europe, Argentina, or the US. Quotes are in euros for Europe and dollars for the Americas, with special pricing for Argentina.</p>
  <p><strong>Scope.</strong> We deliver the diagnosis, the prioritization, and the recommendations. Implementation and the product's compliance stay with whoever builds it. The exception is the full package, which includes support during that implementation.</p>
</div>

